WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.839
Film Courage: Can you talk about how the outer
journey and the inner journey combine within

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:06.770
the 6 stages of plot structure?

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:07.770
Michael Hauge: Yes.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.500
I mean they definitely intertwine.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.280
As I said earlier, you can have a story that
is all outer journey, it’s just plot, it’s

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:23.150
the character doing whatever physically is
needed to achieve that visible goal and there

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.740
is enough conflict from the invisible obstacles,
from nature and other characters that we don’t

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.730
need to go deeper in to the character.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:35.809
But if you choose as a writer to go to that
inner journey level, to explore the inner

00:00:35.809 --> 00:00:42.039
conflict within the hero, to see the tug–of–war
between identity and essence of living a false

00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:46.019
persona and living a character’s truth.

00:00:46.019 --> 00:00:52.570
And living in a fearful state, but protected
state versus living courageously.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:58.909
If you want to explore that, then they will
intertwine because (as I said) the rule is

00:00:58.909 --> 00:01:04.299
the closer they get to achieving, the more
they have to be moving toward their essence.

00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:08.229
It’s called an arc because it arcs over
the whole story.

00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:13.549
And every time their fear takes over they
have to lose ground so to speak.

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:16.609
It keeps them from achieving the goal.

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:22.430
So in a love story this is easy to see because
if the characters are in conflict – the

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.299
hero and what I call the romance character,
the love interest.

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:29.270
If they are arguing, it means one of them
is in his or her identity.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:32.220
And it’s stopping them from getting more
intimate.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.770
If they are both in their essence they’re
going to grow closer and become more intimate.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:42.649
That is why the midpoint, the point of no
return represents a bigger commitment is often

00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:47.999
the first time they make love because it is
physical intimacy that matches the emotional

00:01:47.999 --> 00:01:53.370
intimacy they have from opening up and showing
each other their essence, showing each other

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.830
their truth.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:02:01.280
So that arc for the hero is going to correspond
to the hero getting closer and closer to the

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.940
outer motivation.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.829
But there is another cool way that they can
intertwine or another good tool that you can

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:14.220
use as a screenwriter, or a novelist or filmmaker
of any kind, even actor because this will

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:21.220
inform your performance and that is to look
at some other key characters in the story.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.530
One is the character I term the Reflection.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:28.310
The Reflection is my term for the Sidekick.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:34.970
For the character who is most closely aligned
with the hero, so it might be a best friend,

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:41.160
it might be a co-worker, it might be a mentor
like Mr. Miyagi in THE KARATE KID or if it’s

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.310
the new KARATE KID, Mr. Han. or Obi-Wan.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:54.740
Anyone who is there to support the hero, who
is aligned with the hero at the beginning

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:55.740
of the story.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:01.600
So in GRAVITY for example, Ryan, the Sandra
Bullock character, is the hero of that movie

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:07.060
but is closely aligned with and supported
by the George Clooney character.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:13.980
Now on the visible journey level, on the plot
level, that reflection character is reflection

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:19.260
because their job is to help the hero achieve
her visible goal.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.860
So in GRAVITY, what is her visible goal?

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.060
To get back to earth.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.650
Who is the character who is going to help
her do that more than anyone, it’s George

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:26.650
Clooney’s character.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.980
(I wish I could remember that character’s
name – but I don’t.)

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.700
O.k.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:35.941
But once you have that character functioning
as a reflection on the outer journey, now

00:03:35.941 --> 00:03:39.320
you can see how you use them on the inner
journey level.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:46.320
And on the inner journey level, the reflection
is the character who reveals the hero’s

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.810
essence to the hero.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:53.340
Or another way to say it is, the reflection
is the character that holds the hero’s feet

00:03:53.340 --> 00:04:00.810
to the fire and any time they are retreating
into their identity, the reflection will say

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:01.810
“What are you doing?

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:02.810
This isn’t you?

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.980
You should be going after that person” or
“You can’t give up!”

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:10.790
There are numerous moments in GRAVITY where
the George Clooney character (first as a real

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:15.590
person and then as a figment of her imagination)
says “You don’t want to give up.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:17.730
I know why you want to give up.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:22.430
It’s terrible, it’s terrifying but if
you can find the courage and you can put one

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:27.960
foot in front of the other and keep living
your life, that’s the way you want to be.”

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:33.000
And that is a typical scene or situation for
a reflection character.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.280
Donkey does that for Shrek.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:40.060
The Vince Vaughn character in WEDDING CRASHERS
does it for Owen Wilson’s character.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:44.020
Owen Wilson has retreated once they’ve broke
up into his identity and now they are crashing

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.100
funerals, as well as weddings.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.970
And Vince Vaughn says “Why are you doing
this?

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.370
You’ve got to go after her.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:50.370
She’s going to get married.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.370
She’s marrying the wrong guy.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.370
You love her!”

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:57.080
And so that reflection character can intertwine
on the inner journey because they are sort

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:00.450
of pushing the hero toward their essence.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:05.389
The other character that also becomes the
valuable tool if you’re writing a love story

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:12.490
which is a great genre, it’s a great tool
when you want to explore the inner life of

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:17.590
the character to add a love story to your
plot whatever the genre is…it’s what I

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.300
call the Romance character, the Love Interest.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:26.400
On the visible goal level, if it’s a love
story, the hero’s visible goal has to include

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.560
winning the love of that other character.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.750
They want to end up in a committed relationship
with that person.

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:37.110
They may do it reluctantly, they may be blind
to it at first, but eventually by the mid-point

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:40.980
anyway, they are going to declare their love
in some way and they are going to actively

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.770
pursue that person romantically.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:52.710
But on the inner journey level, the way that
works is the love interest is the reward for

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:58.230
the hero having found the courage to be in
his essence.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.300
So again, you can’t win true love.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.560
You can’t win the love of another character
if you’re living a false life, you have

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.389
to be in your essence.

00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:12.380
And so with love stories, the way it works
is (if you’re writing a love story), to

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.370
avoid the mistake of having these two people
be together just because you want them to

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:23.040
be for no logical reason other than they’re
both good looking and they’re co-stars of

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.040
the movie.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:30.130
If you wanted to really make logical sense
and have your audience respond to it emotionally,

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:35.670
then the romance character is the character
that sees beneath the hero’s identity and

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:38.960
they connect at the level of essence.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:44.270
And so, any time you are in conflict, their
identity is coming into play, when they actually

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.370
connect they are at the level of essence.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:52.419
And nobody else that the hero has actually
fallen for, or is involved with or is in love

00:06:52.419 --> 00:06:57.250
with at the beginning of the movie, none of
them see the hero’s essence, only the true

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.030
love can do that.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:03.270
And so now, that level of plot is also intertwined
with the inner journey as well.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:10.720
Film Courage: Can we talk about a friend who
then becomes an enemy or has been an enemy

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:11.720
all along?

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:15.980
And vice versa, someone who we thought was
an enemy (an opponent) but was actually more

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:19.530
benevolent than we imagined in the beginning
of the movie?

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:26.889
Michael Hauge: Because I use these terms and
this jargon I’ve created like, Hero, Nemesis

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:30.130
(The Villain or Opponent), Romance, Reflection.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:33.050
Because I use those terms, often that question
will come up.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:36.040
Well what if that character starts out as
a Reflection (a Best Friend) and actually

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.900
turns out to be the Killer and then become
the Nemesis?

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:44.560
I recommend a writer not look at it that way.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.840
I recommend that you use the terms that you
would apply at the end of Act One.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:56.550
How is this character established at the beginning
act of your script?

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:00.530
So if this is the character that is the best
friend but is aligned with the hero, than

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:04.419
they remain the Reflection throughout the
movie.

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:09.650
They just become a Reflection who turns out
to be a Bad Guy or a Killer or a Murderer

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:13.139
or a Spy or whatever it turns out that they
are.

00:08:13.139 --> 00:08:18.330
And if a character starts out to be a Friend,
but then this is about the two of them falling

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:22.960
in love and we know that is where it’s headed
then it’s the romance character from the

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.669
get go and there might be another best friend
to be a reflection and so on.

00:08:27.669 --> 00:08:32.229
The Nemesis who turns out to be a Good Guy,
that can happen as well.

00:08:32.229 --> 00:08:34.479
But it’s done slightly differently.

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:40.529
It’s not so much that the Nemesis has been
hiding the fact that he is a Good Guy as much

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:44.619
as he has a change of heart and realizes I
am going after the wrong thing.

00:08:44.619 --> 00:08:45.949
I have to help the hero.

00:08:45.949 --> 00:08:49.339
Tommy Lee Jones in THE FUGITIVE would be that
kind of Nemesis.

00:08:49.339 --> 00:08:53.959
He’s in opposition to the hero but finally
he reaches a point where he realizes I’m

00:08:53.959 --> 00:08:58.050
going after the wrong guy and I’ve got to
help him find the truth.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:03.100
So I don’t think it’s wise to think about
characters changing categories because it

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:08.930
just becomes too complicated and my goal (among
many others) is to make the process as simple

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:10.079
as possible.

00:09:10.079 --> 00:09:14.369
Screenwriting is tough enough without making
it more complicated than it is.

00:09:14.369 --> 00:09:19.199
So I like to use these terms for introducing
characters and how they are going to function

00:09:19.199 --> 00:09:21.070
overall and not start switching categories.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:21.085
QUESTION: Any thoughts you can add on a character’s
inner and outer journey?

